1
00:00:00,301 --> 00:00:02,005
Here are the two investments from the end

2
00:00:02,025 --> 00:00:04,432
of the last video, one in each jar.

3
00:00:05,095 --> 00:00:06,864
Ten thousand dollars at six per cent a

4
00:00:06,905 --> 00:00:08,051
year, for ten years.

5
00:00:08,695 --> 00:00:10,441
The only difference is one phrase.

6
00:00:11,185 --> 00:00:13,448
Simple interest pays six per cent of the

7
00:00:13,590 --> 00:00:16,414
original amount every year, so the account grows

8
00:00:16,475 --> 00:00:18,458
by the same six hundred dollars each time.

9
00:00:19,242 --> 00:00:21,812
That's an arithmetic model, and after ten years

10
00:00:21,954 --> 00:00:23,419
it's sixteen thousand dollars.

11
00:00:24,324 --> 00:00:26,570
Compound interest pays six per cent of the

12
00:00:26,631 --> 00:00:29,440
current balance, so each year multiplies it by

13
00:00:29,581 --> 00:00:30,704
one point zero six.

14
00:00:31,327 --> 00:00:34,032
That's a geometric model, and after ten years

15
00:00:34,193 --> 00:00:36,537
it's seventeen thousand, nine hundred and eight dollars,

16
00:00:36,678 --> 00:00:37,158
forty-eight.

17
00:00:38,301 --> 00:00:40,528
Same numbers, different words, different model.

18
00:00:41,492 --> 00:00:43,460
So which words justify which model?

19
00:00:44,469 --> 00:00:46,176
Here are the finance phrases to look for.

20
00:00:46,979 --> 00:00:49,463
The same amount added each period means an

21
00:00:49,645 --> 00:00:51,448
arithmetic model, a straight line.

22
00:00:52,191 --> 00:00:54,717
The same percentage of the current amount means

23
00:00:54,799 --> 00:00:57,827
a geometric model, multiplying by one plus r

24
00:00:57,848 --> 00:00:58,609
each period.

25
00:00:59,673 --> 00:01:02,795
Equal end-of-period deposits into an account that starts

26
00:01:02,816 --> 00:01:05,458
at zero add up to a geometric series,

27
00:01:05,939 --> 00:01:08,481
so that's an annuity, with future value M

28
00:01:08,682 --> 00:01:10,723
times one plus r to the n, minus

29
00:01:10,844 --> 00:01:12,064
one, all over r.

30
00:01:12,966 --> 00:01:15,509
And equal repayments on a loan, where interest

31
00:01:15,570 --> 00:01:17,793
is charged on the balance, give the loan

32
00:01:17,834 --> 00:01:20,076
model, with the balance zero when it's paid

33
00:01:20,157 --> 00:01:20,256
off.

34
00:01:21,119 --> 00:01:21,859
In every one,

35
00:01:22,442 --> 00:01:24,304
r is the rate for one period, and

36
00:01:24,566 --> 00:01:25,707
n counts the periods.

37
00:01:26,891 --> 00:01:29,317
Which model does "can afford a repayment each

38
00:01:29,378 --> 00:01:30,159
month" point to?

39
00:01:30,945 --> 00:01:31,749
Think for three seconds.

40
00:01:32,855 --> 00:01:34,660
It's a loan with equal repayments.

41
00:01:35,958 --> 00:01:36,763
So here's that question.

42
00:01:37,346 --> 00:01:39,331
A buyer can afford two thousand eight hundred

43
00:01:39,372 --> 00:01:41,157
dollars a month, at four point eight per

44
00:01:41,178 --> 00:01:44,105
cent a year, compounded monthly, for twenty-five years.

45
00:01:44,709 --> 00:01:46,124
What's the most they can borrow?

46
00:01:47,072 --> 00:01:47,492
Per month,

47
00:01:47,996 --> 00:01:50,465
r is point zero zero four, and n

48
00:01:50,587 --> 00:01:51,309
is three hundred.

49
00:01:52,073 --> 00:01:53,879
The loan is paid off after the last

50
00:01:53,940 --> 00:01:56,448
repayment, so set the balance after three hundred

51
00:01:56,489 --> 00:01:57,391
months to zero.

52
00:01:58,157 --> 00:02:00,510
The unknown this time is P, the amount

53
00:02:00,551 --> 00:02:00,913
borrowed.

54
00:02:01,876 --> 00:02:03,958
Move the repayments' part to the other side

55
00:02:04,119 --> 00:02:06,862
and divide, and P is about four hundred

56
00:02:06,883 --> 00:02:09,586
and eighty-eight thousand, six hundred and fifty-eight dollars,

57
00:02:09,767 --> 00:02:10,447
seventy-nine.

58
00:02:11,089 --> 00:02:13,794
Because it's a maximum, round down, to seventy-eight

59
00:02:13,815 --> 00:02:14,075
cents,

60
00:02:14,617 --> 00:02:16,380
so the repayments are sure to clear it.

61
00:02:17,123 --> 00:02:19,488
Multiplying two thousand eight hundred by three hundred

62
00:02:19,569 --> 00:02:20,591
is the classic slip.

63
00:02:21,113 --> 00:02:23,811
That's the total repaid, including all the interest.

64
00:02:24,441 --> 00:02:26,330
Probability questions work the same way.

65
00:02:26,932 --> 00:02:28,899
If the question lists the values with their

66
00:02:28,919 --> 00:02:32,692
probabilities, it's a discrete distribution, and the probabilities

67
00:02:32,852 --> 00:02:33,434
add to one.

68
00:02:34,177 --> 00:02:36,866
If it gives a density function, probabilities are

69
00:02:37,046 --> 00:02:39,555
areas under it, and the total area is

70
00:02:39,695 --> 00:02:39,795
one.

71
00:02:40,678 --> 00:02:43,147
If equal-width parts of an interval are equally

72
00:02:43,227 --> 00:02:45,154
likely, the density is flat,

73
00:02:45,856 --> 00:02:47,060
one over the width of the interval.

74
00:02:47,683 --> 00:02:49,825
And if it says normally distributed, with a

75
00:02:49,886 --> 00:02:52,349
mean and a standard deviation, the curve is

76
00:02:52,390 --> 00:02:55,074
a bell, and the empirical rule or z-scores

77
00:02:55,395 --> 00:02:56,336
give the probabilities.

78
00:02:57,180 --> 00:02:57,743
Here's a test.

79
00:02:58,387 --> 00:03:00,732
A question asks how likely a delivery is

80
00:03:00,753 --> 00:03:02,617
to take more than fifty-eight minutes.

81
00:03:03,280 --> 00:03:04,005
Which model fits?

82
00:03:04,813 --> 00:03:05,577
Think for three seconds.

83
00:03:06,385 --> 00:03:07,376
You can't tell yet.

84
00:03:07,782 --> 00:03:09,776
The question has to say which shape it

85
00:03:09,837 --> 00:03:10,118
means.

86
00:03:11,148 --> 00:03:13,791
First, suppose the time is uniform from forty

87
00:03:13,872 --> 00:03:14,592
to sixty minutes,

88
00:03:15,114 --> 00:03:17,596
so equal stretches of time are equally likely.

89
00:03:18,300 --> 00:03:20,714
The density is flat, at one over twenty.

90
00:03:21,439 --> 00:03:23,505
More than fifty-eight minutes is the last two

91
00:03:23,546 --> 00:03:25,832
minutes of the twenty, so the probability is

92
00:03:25,913 --> 00:03:27,938
two twentieths, which is one in ten.

93
00:03:28,881 --> 00:03:31,525
Now suppose instead the times are normally distributed,

94
00:03:31,866 --> 00:03:34,530
with mean fifty and standard deviation four.

95
00:03:35,252 --> 00:03:38,039
Fifty-eight is two standard deviations above the mean.

96
00:03:38,621 --> 00:03:40,726
About ninety-five per cent of times are within

97
00:03:40,807 --> 00:03:43,233
two standard deviations, so about five per cent

98
00:03:43,334 --> 00:03:45,760
are outside, and half of that is above.

99
00:03:46,443 --> 00:03:47,910
That's about two point five per cent.

100
00:03:48,675 --> 00:03:51,590
Same threshold, different shape, so a different answer.

101
00:03:52,355 --> 00:03:54,105
And the normal model isn't even limited to

102
00:03:54,206 --> 00:03:54,990
forty to sixty.

103
00:03:55,514 --> 00:03:57,842
The normal curve piles its probability near the

104
00:03:57,863 --> 00:04:00,271
mean, and leaves only a thin tail out

105
00:04:00,292 --> 00:04:00,492
there.

106
00:04:01,648 --> 00:04:02,094
One more pair.

107
00:04:02,760 --> 00:04:04,843
In the first question, a spinner lands on

108
00:04:05,044 --> 00:04:07,988
one, two, three or four, with probability k

109
00:04:08,168 --> 00:04:08,209
x.

110
00:04:10,111 --> 00:04:10,612
In the second,

111
00:04:11,133 --> 00:04:12,855
X is a choc chip's distance from the

112
00:04:12,875 --> 00:04:15,178
centre of a cookie, in centimetres, and it

113
00:04:15,218 --> 00:04:18,182
has density k x, from zero to four.

114
00:04:19,007 --> 00:04:20,820
Same rule, but is k the same?

115
00:04:21,645 --> 00:04:23,932
For the list, the four probabilities add to

116
00:04:24,153 --> 00:04:24,253
one.

117
00:04:24,815 --> 00:04:27,642
That's k plus two k plus three k

118
00:04:27,783 --> 00:04:30,048
plus four k, which is ten k,

119
00:04:30,551 --> 00:04:31,913
so k is one tenth.

120
00:04:32,857 --> 00:04:35,387
For the density, the area under k x

121
00:04:35,448 --> 00:04:37,014
from zero to four is one.

122
00:04:37,616 --> 00:04:39,978
That's a triangle, half of four times four

123
00:04:40,039 --> 00:04:42,722
k, which is eight k, so k is

124
00:04:42,843 --> 00:04:45,306
one eighth, the cookie's density from the earlier

125
00:04:45,327 --> 00:04:45,586
video.

126
00:04:46,468 --> 00:04:48,394
A sum of bars and an area under

127
00:04:48,435 --> 00:04:50,783
a line are different totals, so they give

128
00:04:50,864 --> 00:04:51,867
different values of k.

129
00:04:52,523 --> 00:04:54,441
Here's the habit for every model question.

130
00:04:54,924 --> 00:04:56,933
The words pick the model, and the numbers

131
00:04:57,054 --> 00:04:57,637
just fill it in.

132
00:04:58,343 --> 00:04:59,416
Same amount, add.

133
00:04:59,882 --> 00:05:01,360
Same percentage, multiply.

134
00:05:01,865 --> 00:05:03,094
A list sums to one.

135
00:05:03,619 --> 00:05:05,096
A density has area one.

136
00:05:07,664 --> 00:05:08,117
Your turn.

137
00:05:08,902 --> 00:05:11,345
Two thousand dollars is deposited at the end

138
00:05:11,385 --> 00:05:13,768
of each year into a new, empty account

139
00:05:14,048 --> 00:05:16,792
earning five per cent a year, compounded annually.

140
00:05:17,534 --> 00:05:20,042
Find the value of the account immediately after

141
00:05:20,082 --> 00:05:21,225
the tenth deposit.

142
00:05:21,807 --> 00:05:22,837
Pause here to answer.

143
00:05:33,541 --> 00:05:37,131
Equal end-of-year deposits, starting from zero, make an

144
00:05:37,191 --> 00:05:37,632
annuity.

145
00:05:38,215 --> 00:05:39,744
The last one goes in at the end,

146
00:05:39,945 --> 00:05:41,031
so it earns nothing yet.

147
00:05:41,775 --> 00:05:45,262
So the value is two thousand, times one

148
00:05:45,322 --> 00:05:47,586
point zero five to the ten, minus one,

149
00:05:48,147 --> 00:05:49,971
all over point zero five.

150
00:05:50,674 --> 00:05:53,548
That's about twenty-five thousand, one hundred and fifty-five

151
00:05:53,568 --> 00:05:54,633
dollars, seventy-nine.

152
00:05:55,597 --> 00:05:58,424
Thirty-two thousand, five hundred and seventy-seven dollars, eighty-nine

153
00:05:58,466 --> 00:05:58,886
is wrong.

154
00:05:59,368 --> 00:06:01,515
It treats all ten deposits as if they

155
00:06:01,556 --> 00:06:02,639
went in at the start.

156
00:06:04,390 --> 00:06:04,892
Last one.

157
00:06:05,658 --> 00:06:08,000
Bags of chips are filled by a machine,

158
00:06:08,581 --> 00:06:11,344
and their masses are normally distributed, with mean

159
00:06:11,505 --> 00:06:14,747
five hundred grams and standard deviation five grams.

160
00:06:15,570 --> 00:06:18,036
Use the empirical rule to estimate the percentage

161
00:06:18,077 --> 00:06:20,683
of bags under four hundred and ninety grams.

162
00:06:21,267 --> 00:06:22,100
Pause here to answer.

163
00:06:32,841 --> 00:06:35,306
Four hundred and ninety is ten grams below

164
00:06:35,346 --> 00:06:37,590
the mean, which is two standard deviations.

165
00:06:38,292 --> 00:06:40,660
About ninety-five per cent of bags are within

166
00:06:40,781 --> 00:06:43,630
two standard deviations, so about five per cent

167
00:06:43,730 --> 00:06:44,252
are outside.

168
00:06:45,074 --> 00:06:47,480
Only the lower tail counts, so it's about

169
00:06:47,560 --> 00:06:48,643
two point five per cent.

170
00:06:49,589 --> 00:06:50,520
Five per cent is wrong.

171
00:06:50,885 --> 00:06:52,979
It counts both tails, the heavy bags as

172
00:06:53,021 --> 00:06:53,221
well.

173
00:06:55,382 --> 00:06:58,109
So in every model question, read the words

174
00:06:58,270 --> 00:06:59,192
before the numbers.

175
00:07:00,074 --> 00:07:01,996
That's the last of the practice videos, and

176
00:07:02,016 --> 00:07:03,979
the end of the Year 12 Advanced course.

177
00:07:04,680 --> 00:07:07,187
Here are the three habits, one for differentiation,

178
00:07:07,568 --> 00:07:09,453
one for integration and one for models.

179
00:07:12,688 --> 00:07:13,530
Here are the key notes.

180
00:07:14,153 --> 00:07:15,701
Pause the video if you need more time

